  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 232 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nSaturday and beyond, north and central Georgia will remain within a \nregime of northwesterly flow aloft, with weakening high pressure at \nthe surface in the wake of a northeastward-advancing coastal low. \nMultiple pushes of supremely dry, Canadian continental air will \nrotate around aforementioned low and into the Southeast, with \nPWATs progged to fall between the 10th and 25th percentiles \nclimatologically (generally less than three-quarters of an inch, \nwith more seasonable values expected on Sunday). The combination \nof uncharacteristically dry air and subsidence from lingering \nsurface high pressure will keep PoPs near-negligible through \nmidweek next week (<15%, which is the delineator between dry and a\nmention of isolated rain chances). Moderation of temperatures \nwill occur as the airmass stagnates, with afternoon highs rising \nback into the 80s areawide -- back to 5 to 10 degrees above \naverage by next Tuesday. Despite this, morning lows will be crisp \nand positively fall-like (finally), in the 50s to lower 60s for \nmost.",
